# Copy to data/keys.env (or set REACHOUT_KEYS) and fill in. Gitignored; a real environment variable
# always wins over anything set here.
#
# Search is load-bearing: staff-directory, public-documents and public-pages
# cannot find anything without it. Configure at least one keyed backend - the
# keyless DuckDuckGo fallback rate-limits after two or three queries and a
# single lookup makes five or six.

# Serper - https://serper.dev/  (recommended primary)
# A Google SERP passthrough: `site:`, `filetype:pdf`, quoted phrases and OR
# groups reach Google unchanged, which is what the staff-directory and
# public-documents queries are built on. Semantic search APIs (Tavily, Exa)
# quietly ignore those operators, so they are not usable as a backend here.
# 2,500 free queries on signup with no card. After that it is PREPAID credit
# packs - $50 for 50,000 queries - so there is no open billing relationship and
# nothing to settle at the end of the month. At ~5-6 queries per lookup, the
# free allowance is roughly 400 lookups.
SERPER_API_KEY=

# SearXNG - a metasearch instance you host yourself. No key, no quota, no
# account, and a far better last resort than the DuckDuckGo scraper below.
# It forwards each query to Google/Bing/DuckDuckGo and merges the results, so
# operators survive as far as whichever upstream handles them.
#   1. docker run -d -p 8080:8080 -v ./searxng:/etc/searxng \
#        -e BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/ docker.io/searxng/searxng:latest
#   2. In the settings.yml it writes to ./searxng, add `json` to the formats
#      list (it ships with only html, and the API 403s without it):
#        search:
#          formats:
#            - html
#            - json
#   3. Restart the container, then set the URL below.
# Self-hosting means your own IP does the talking, so a heavy run can get the
# upstream engines throttling you. ReachOut reads SearXNG's
# `unresponsive_engines` field and reports that as an outage rather than as
# "nothing published".
SEARXNG_URL=

# Brave Search API - https://api-dashboard.search.brave.com/
# Postpaid only: $5.00 per 1,000 requests, with $5 of credit granted every month
# (~1,000 requests/month at no charge). There is no free tier any more, so an
# account with billing set up is required even to spend nothing but the credit.
# Since GOOGLE_CSE below is closed to new signups, this is now the only keyed
# backend a new user can add besides Serper - and the card is required even
# though the monthly credit covers ~1,000 requests at no actual cost.
BRAVE_API_KEY=

# Google Programmable Search + Custom Search JSON API
# CLOSED TO NEW CUSTOMERS since 2025, and shutting down entirely on 1 Jan 2027.
# A new Cloud project gets 403 PERMISSION_DENIED even with the API enabled, so
# there is no point attempting the setup below unless you already hold a working
# key. Google points new users at Vertex AI Search, which is enterprise-priced
# and returns site search rather than open-web results - not a substitute here.
# If you have an existing key it still works until the shutdown date; plan on
# SearXNG or Serper carrying the load after that.
#   1. Create the engine at https://programmablesearchengine.google.com/
#   2. The CX is the `cx=` value in the engine's snippet.
#   3. Enable "Custom Search API" in Google Cloud console and create an API key.
GOOGLE_CSE_KEY=
GOOGLE_CSE_CX=

# Set to 1 ONLY if the engine really does search the whole web (the "Search the
# entire web" toggle is not offered on every account). Leave it off for an
# engine restricted to a list of sites: a restricted index finding nothing says
# nothing about the open web, so its empty results are allowed to fall through
# to the next backend rather than ending the search.
#
# Good sites to list for phone discovery, best first:
#   prnewswire.com businesswire.com globenewswire.com   press releases end in a
#                                                       named "Media Contact"
#                                                       block with a real number
#   muckrack.com speakerhub.com f6s.com                 profiles built around
#                                                       being contactable
#   springer.com elsevier.com nature.com                corresponding-author
#   sciencedirect.com plos.org                          contact blocks
#   github.com gitlab.com gravatar.com speakerdeck.com  profiles linking to a
#   slideshare.net ted.com                              personal site
# Low yield for phones: stackoverflow.com npmjs.com kaggle.com dribbble.com
#   behance.net indiehackers.com
# linkedin.com is fine to list - the snippet is used as a lead, but the page is
# never fetched (their terms forbid it; see docs/ETHICS.md).
GOOGLE_CSE_WHOLE_WEB=0

# GitHub - raises the API limit from 60/hour to 5,000/hour.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
